  <div class="fstep"><strong>2026.</strong> The petitioners finally move the High Court — about five years after the FIR, four after the Sessions rejection. On 5 November 2026, Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Alok Kumar Sinha declines to entertain the application.</div>
</div>
<p>The order&rsquo;s reasoning is compact: the petitioners could not explain the delay, and the record showed there had never been any real apprehension of arrest. The Court did not discuss the merits of the allegations at all — delay and conduct were sufficient.</p>

<h2>Why delay is fatal in this jurisdiction</h2>
<p>Anticipatory bail under Section 438 CrPC — now Section 482 of the BNSS — is a shield against an imminent threat to liberty. Its grant turns on a live apprehension, assessed on the day of hearing. Years of inaction tell the court one of two things, and neither helps the applicant: either the police never sought the applicant (so there is no apprehension to protect against), or the applicant evaded the process and now seeks the court&rsquo;s indulgence as a matter of convenience. Courts are also alert to a third pattern — applications timed not to any fresh threat but to a procedural milestone in the trial, such as the issuance of process or a warrant, which is a different problem requiring a different remedy.</p>
<p>None of this makes delay an absolute bar. An applicant who can show the case lay dormant, that no process was ever issued, and that a fresh, identifiable development revived the threat of arrest — a supplementary chargesheet, a newly issued warrant, a revived investigation — can still succeed. What the law refuses to reward is silence: a gap of years with no affidavit explaining it.</p>

<h2>Lessons for the defence brief</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Move promptly after a Sessions Court rejection; the High Court reads the interval as evidence about the reality of the apprehension.</p>
  <p>If delay exists, explain it on affidavit with dates — illness, absence from the jurisdiction, dormancy of the investigation — rather than leaving the court to infer evasion.</p>
  <p>Do not lean on parity alone. Co-accused orders help only applicants whose own conduct is clean; parity supplements, never substitutes, a maintainable plea.</p>
  <p>Where process has already been issued in the interval, consider whether the correct remedy is now regular bail on surrender, or an application addressing the warrant, rather than anticipatory bail.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information. It does not comment on the pending investigation or trial, and the law on anticipatory bail is applied to the facts of each case.</div>
